{%- comment -%}
  The size chart — a real table built from theme settings, not a pasted blob.

  Asked for 2026-09-13 with a screenshot of a competitor's size-guide panel:
  a table from XS to 3XL and a cm/inch switch. Two things about that screenshot
  were deliberately NOT copied, and both were agreed first:

  - **The COLUMNS are trousers, not the screenshot's.** The reference table was
    a top's — body length back, shoulder width seam to seam, body width, sleeve
    length centre back. Sixteen of the twenty products left on this store after
    the sold-out cull are trousers and culottes, and a shopper buying trousers
    measures a waist, not a shoulder. Shipping a top's chart on Yora Loose Pants
    would have been a wrong measurement stated confidently, which is the one
    class of content this repo refuses. The studio chose the format over the
    numbers; the numbers are theirs to send.
  - **It is inline, not a modal.** The same instruction that asked for this chart
    asked for the product page's four tabs to become one page. A dialog would
    have put a second thing to open back on a page that just had one taken off.

  WHERE THE DATA LIVES, and why it is a theme setting rather than a metafield.
  `custom.size_chart` still exists and still wins where a product has its own —
  see `product-detail.liquid`. What this renders is the SHARED chart, because
  Wear Label has one size system and eleven products cut to it: a metafield
  would mean the same table typed eleven times and eleven places to correct when
  one number is wrong. A theme setting is one field, in the theme editor, where
  every other piece of copy on this site already lives.

  THE SHAPE IS ONE TEXTAREA, ONE ROW PER LINE, PIPE SEPARATED:

      XS | 64 | 90 | 28 | 72 | 26
      S  | 68 | 94 | 28 | 72 | 27

  which is chosen so the studio can see the whole table at once and so that
  adding a size is a line, not a code change. It also keeps the size names out
  of this file — XS and 3XL are data, and a hardcoded user-visible string in a
  snippet is the rule this theme breaks least often.

  A ragged row is rendered ragged rather than padded or dropped. A missing cell
  is a number nobody has supplied, and silently filling it with an empty string
  the reader cannot tell from a measured zero is worse than a short row.

  CM TO INCHES IS COMPUTED, NOT TYPED. Every numeric cell carries `data-cm` and
  assets/theme.js rewrites the text when the switch moves; the studio types one
  set of numbers. Without script the switch is removed by the `scripting: none`
  block in theme-src/theme.css and the table stays in centimetres, which is the
  unit the studio measures in and the one the numbers are typed in.

  The wrapper is `.wl-table`, the same class the rich-text metafield route uses,
  so a chart from either source is styled once and scrolls sideways on a phone
  rather than forcing horizontal page scroll.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ROWS ARE SPLIT WITH `newline_to_br`, NOT ON A NEWLINE LITERAL, and that is not
  a style choice. A `{% liquid %}` block is line-based: a quoted string carrying
  a real line break inside one ends the tag early, and theme check reports it as
  "expected end of input" pointing at the comment above rather than at the split.
  It cost a round trip here. `newline_to_br` turns the breaks into markup that
  can be split on a single line; both spellings are handled because the filter's
  output has been written each way.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign columns = settings.size_guide_columns | split: ','

  assign row_source = settings.size_guide_rows | strip | newline_to_br
  assign row_source = row_source | replace: '<br />', '|~|' | replace: '<br>', '|~|'
  assign rows = row_source | split: '|~|'

  assign has_rows = false
  if settings.size_guide_rows != blank and rows.size > 0
    assign has_rows = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if has_rows -%}
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    {%- comment -%}
      Radios rather than two buttons: the unit is one choice out of two, which is
      what a radio group is, and it arrives keyboard-operable with arrow keys for
      free. `data-unit-switch` is the hook; the fieldset is what `scripting: none`
      removes.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <fieldset data-unit-switch class="flex items-center gap-5">
      <legend class="sr-only">{{ 'product.units' | t }}</legend>

      <label class="inline-flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-muted has-checked:text-ink">
        <input type="radio" name="size-unit" value="cm" checked class="size-4 accent-brand">
        {{ 'product.cm' | t }}
      </label>

      <label class="inline-flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-muted has-checked:text-ink">
        <input type="radio" name="size-unit" value="in" class="size-4 accent-brand">
        {{ 'product.inch' | t }}
      </label>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="wl-table">
      <table data-size-table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            {%- for column in columns -%}
              <th scope="col">{{ column | strip }}</th>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {%- for row in rows -%}
            {%- assign cells = row | split: '|' -%}
            <tr>
              {%- for cell in cells -%}
                {%- assign value = cell | strip -%}
                {%- if forloop.first -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    The first cell is the size name, so it is a row header and it
                    is never converted — "XS" is not a measurement.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <th scope="row">{{ value }}</th>
                {%- else -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    `data-cm` is what makes the cell convertible. A cell that is
                    not a number keeps none, so script leaves it alone — a dash or
                    a note in the middle of a table survives the switch.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    EVERY measurement cell carries `data-cm`, and the decision
                    about whether it converts is left to script. The first pass
                    gated this on `value | times: 1 > 0`, which looks right and
                    silently breaks a RANGE: "62-90" casts to 62, so the cell
                    would have been relabelled 24.4 in inches and the range would
                    have been thrown away. theme.js rewrites each number it finds
                    inside the string instead, so "62-90" becomes "24.4-35.4" and
                    a cell holding a dash or a note is left alone because it
                    holds no digits at all.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <td data-cm="{{ value }}">{{ value }}</td>
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </tr>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {%- if settings.size_guide_note != blank -%}
      <p class="text-caption leading-snug text-ink-muted">{{ settings.size_guide_note }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No rows typed yet. The labelled placeholder at final size, like every other
    empty slot on this site — the layout is finished before the numbers arrive.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="wl-table">
    {% render 'copy', value: blank, label: 'size chart', lines: 4 %}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
